{"id": "d20c9914-6410-4b82-861d-e07acc2083c7", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:37:14.339015", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "438e1238-a4a1-4fd1-b55f-6156fd2d432d", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: absolutely nothing besides task.json. No model weights, no datasets, no repos.\n\nResearcher topics: neurips2024/94328/4:setup_5, setup_7, setup_11, setup_16, setup_17 — presumably references to the QuaRot NeurIPS 2024 paper setups. I can't access those sources (no network, no local docs). I should note that as an uncertainty and possibly frame questions on those topics (question_budget 6). But the researcher only asked for inspection + proposal. I could propose up to a few clarifying questions keyed to the topics, to be asked later or included as open items.\n\nLet me now write the plan response in Spanish (researcher wrote in Spanish).\n\nPlan content:\n\n1) Inspección:\n- /workspace contains only task.json (579 bytes, 8K total). No git repos, no scripts, no notebooks, no configs, no requirements.txt, no environment.yml, no data dirs.\n- Filesystem-wide search for quarot/gptq/llama/wikitext/track.md: no matches. track.md exists only as a document string inside task.json: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- Models/datasets: none present. No HF cache (/home/actor/.cache empty, HOME effectively no cache), no datasets dir, task.json \"datasets\": [] in environment. So: no LLaMA-2/3, no OPT, no WikiText-2, no C4 artifacts locally. Any model/dataset reference must be nominal (design-only), sourced from paper knowledge, marked as assumptions to be confirmed via researcher topics.\n- Environment: Python 3.14.4 at /usr/bin/python3, pip 25.1.1 available but network blocked (DNS resolution fails → cannot install). Zero third-party packages: torch, transformers, datasets, numpy, scipy, accelerate, lm_eval, bitsandbytes, triton etc. all MISSING (even numpy). No GPU (/dev/nvidia* absent), CPU affinity 224 cores.\n- Implication: pure design deliverable. No quantization code, no perplexity evaluation can be executed. Feasible checks limited to: static reasoning, design-internal consistency checks (e.g., bits/accounting arithmetic, matrix-shape/Hadamard dimension checks, cost estimates computed analytically). We must not claim runs. Also note Python 3.14 would be a practical incompatibility risk for pinned quantization stacks (QuRot repo uses older PyTorch) — a design note for reproducibility instructions, marked SUPUESTO.\n\n2) Propuesta (one paragraph each):\n- Diseño comparativo: factorial 3 métodos × 3 bits × N modelos, same checkpoints, same eval data. Arms: RTN (round-to-nearest, per-channel/per-group symmetric or asymmetric), GPTQ (Hessian-based second-order, calibration required), and each with/without QuaRot Hadamard rotations → 2×3×|models|. Key: QuaRot applied to weight-only path = offline Hadamard on weights (H_d on input dimension of each linear) + online Hadamard on activation inputs (for the \"activation rotated online\" trick) — for weight-only W-only we mainly use the pairwise/offline Hadamard to smooth weight outliers; but QuaRot's full recipe rotates residual stream and K-V cache, which mainly affects activation/KV quantization. So define an ablation ladder: A0 RTN, A1 GPTQ, A2 RTN+H_w (offline weight Hadamard), A3 GPTQ+H_w, A4 GPTQ+H_w+H_online (full QuaRot weight-side), optionally A5 +A8 activation/KV to show it's orthogonal. That answers \"does QuaRot stabilize/improve W-only 2–4 bit\".\n- Control variables: identical fp16 checkpoint, tokenizer, eval split (WikiText-2 test with fixed seq len 2048, C4 validation), same group size, same symmetric/asymmetric scheme, same rounding granularity, same calibration set (128 samples × 2048 tokens from C4, SUPUESTO matching paper), same seeds, same layer subset (all nn.Linear except explicitly-excluded first/last blocks and outliers kept FP16 for all arms including baseline, so any gain is attributable to rotation, not to fewer quantized params), same effective bits accounting.\n- Métricas: primary = WikiText-2 perplexity (and C4 ppl) per (model, bits, arm), plus Δ ppl vs fp16 reference and \"degradation ratio\" Δ/100; secondary = zero-shot accuracy on a fixed suite (e.g., PiQA, ARC-easy, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, LAMBADA, boolq → average), exact-match vs fp16 top-1 agreement, and quantization diagnostics: MSE between quantized and fp weight per layer, weight outlier statistics (max|w|/rms) before/after Hadamard, and KL between quantized and fp next-token distributions on eval tokens (measures stabilization without relying on accuracy noise). Plus 3 seeds, and quality-vs-bitbudget curve at 4/3/2 bits with \"collapse threshold\" defined as first bit-width where Δ ppl > threshold (e.g., 10% relative to fp16) — pre-registered.\n- Cost/feasibility: since no GPU here, the design specifies hardware and estimates analytically; propose scaling down (e.g., 1B-2B models for the full grid, 7B for the headline, 70B optional single seed).\n\n3) Supuestos/uncertainties, each marked SUPUESTO. And the researcher topics — map them: setup_5/7/11/16/17 likely cover model family, bit settings, calibration set, group size, rotation targets; I should ask targeted questions (budget 6) about those. Actually, the topics are what the researcher can clarify. I'll list the specific questions I need answered, keyed to topics, within budget of 6 questions.\n\nNote W ∈ {4,3,2} is a fixed requirement.\n\nAlso flag: QuaRot's headline claim is 4-bit for weights AND activations AND KV cache; the question restricts to weight-only. So a genuine scientific uncertainty: QuaRot's benefit in weight-only-only setting is smaller than in full 4-bit inference because the main outlier problem is in activations. That's a key framing — the design should isolate the weight-side benefit and be honest that if only weights are quantized, Hadamard rotation of weights (offline, orthogonal transform on input dim) changes rounding geometry and can only help by smoothing weight outliers; rotation of activations is irrelevant unless activations are quantized. Important nuance: for weight-only quantization, applying an orthogonal H to the input dimension of W must be compensated by H^T on the input activations (X → X H^T) to preserve the function — which requires either fusing H into the preceding layer (offline, if it's a linear op without non-linearity, e.g., layernorm folding) or computing online. So the design must specify how compensation is preserved (identity pair trick). Good technical content.\n\nAlso: Hadamard requires the input dimension to be a power of 2 (or use randomized block-diagonal pairwise Hadamard for non-power-of-2 dims); LLaMA dims 4096/11008/... 11008 = 2^7*86? Actually 11008 = 2^7 × 86 = 128×86; not a power of two → QuaRot uses randomized Hadamard with block decomposition or padding. This is a design detail/uncertainty, and a feasible check: arithmetic divisibility check computable offline (we could actually compute this). That's a legitimate \"feasible check\" for the design stage — e.g., compute per-layer dims for LLaMA-2-7B/13B/70B and check power-of-2 property, and compute model size accounting for group sizes at 2/3/4 bits (bits/weight overhead). Those can be done analytically with pure Python. Good.\n\nLet me maybe run a small pure-python arithmetic check now as a \"feasible check\" example — e.g., effective bits with group size g: bits + 16/g (scale) + maybe 16/g for zero → and memory per model. And check Hadamard dims for LLaMA-2 7B (hidden 4096, ffn 11008, heads 32, head_dim 128), 13B (5120, 13824), 70B (8192, 28672), LLaMA-3-8B (4096, 14336), 70B (8192, 28672), OPT-1.3B/2.7B/6.7B/13B (2048/2560/4096/5120 hidden; ffn 4×hidden). Let me compute the power-of-2 status. That's cheap and demonstrates value. But careful: these dims are from memory → mark as SUPUESTO (need confirmation). Fine — the check is about arithmetic design implications.\n\nI'll run one quick pure-python computation to include