Recommend: primary = block-diagonal with block size aligned to group boundary (B=128 = g), so group boundaries are preserved and effective bits identical; secondary = global Hadamard for the two main models. Hmm, but with B=g=128, a Hadamard of size 128 mixes exactly within one group; each group becomes independently rotated → equivalent to per-group smoothing. This is nice and auditable, and preserves the group-locality of scales. Let's propose primary: block-diag H with B = 128 aligned to groups (and B=64/256 for the g-sweep, i.e., always B=g so alignment holds). Secondary sensitivity: B=512 or global on LLaMA-2-7B. Good, that's a concrete, defensible design choice.\n\nAlso: which weight matrices get rotation. In weight-only, a rotation R1 folded into W_q,W_k,W_v,W_up,W_gate (input dim = d_model, after LN) requires the LN output x to be pre-multiplied by R1^T online. That's an online FLOP on activations. Alternative fully-offline-equivalent: since activation is FP16-exact, we could equivalently fold R1^T into the preceding matrices (W_o, W_down, embeddings) — this is exactly how QuaRot folds offline: W_o' = R1 W_o (output dim rotation) etc. So for weight-only, folding R1 into residual stream means rotating OUTPUT dims of W_o, W_down and embeddings — which does not change quantization grid at all for group-along-input quantization (output-dim rotation is a permutation of rows; row rotations are orthogonal mixes across rows — that DOES change weights values, but group quant along input dim: rotating output dim mixes different output channels, each of which has its own scale... changes values). Hmm. The important thing for weight-only: rotation matters only when applied along the INPUT dimension of the matrix being quantized (columns in xW convention), because that's where the group's quantization error gets shaped and because the compensating online transform is on activations.\n\nSo the traceability table should mark: for each rotation R1..R5, for each weight matrix: acts along input dim (affects quantization grid → KEEP, quantize post-rotation weights) vs acts along output dim (does not change quantization difficulty per se but must be applied so that the compensated input-side rotation is exact → keep as exact FP folding). And online activation rotations R4 (attn out before o_proj — input dim of o_proj! so it affects o_proj quantization grid: quantize W_o · H^T, apply H online to attn output), R5 online before down_proj (input dim of down_proj = d_ff → affects W_down grid, apply H online to activation). Great — so in weight-only, R4/R5 (the \"online\" Hadamards) are precisely the ones that DO change the quantization grid of o_proj and down_proj. And K-V cache rotations R2/R3 are irrelevant in weight-only (cache is FP16) → anulled by cancellation.\n\nLet me structure:\nWeight matrices and their input dims for LLaMA:\n- q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, up_proj, gate_proj: input dim = d_model → rotation R1 (H_d) folded offline into these weights, compensated by online R1^T on LN output... but wait, if we instead fold R1^T into the preceding linear's output side (W_o, W_down) — for weight-only, that keeps everything offline but then the input dim of W_o/W_down (which are quantized) also gets rotated (o_proj input = n_h·d_h → R2/R4 domain; down_proj input = d_ff → R5 domain). This is the elegant QuaRot invariant structure: R1 applied on residual stream is offline-foldable (embeddings, LN folding, and W_o/W_down right-multiplied by R1^T), so no online op needed for R1. And the online-only ones are R4 (after softmax, non-linear → must be online) and R5 (after SiLU, non-linear → must be online). And R2/R3 relate to head structure and K/V cache.\n\nSo the weight-only design should keep: R1 (offline fold both sides — affects quantization grids of W_o? no...). Ugh, careful. Let me lay out the algebra cleanly for the design doc:\n\nFor a quantized weight W ∈ R^{out×in} with grouping along `in`: the quantization error depends on the distribution