adamard: H = diag blocks, or a \"randomized Hadamard\" H = H_d ∘ random signs which requires d = power of 2. Actually the standard workaround: apply Hadamard of size largest power-of-2 divisor, block-diagonal. For d = 11008 = 256·43, use block-diag of 43 blocks of H_256. That's what I'll propose. Padding approach: pad to 16384 with zeros and apply H_16384 then slice — the resulting transform on the original coordinates is P H P^T restricted... that is not orthogonal on R^d (rows/cols truncated), so it doesn't preserve exactness of the rotation identity → fidelity loss. Whereas block-diagonal with B | d is exactly orthogonal → preserves model output up to floating point. That's a crucial argument: block-diagonal is exact (H H^T = I holds), padded dense is not. Great, that's the argument for the choice with a fidelity statement: block-diag only mixes within-block coordinates → weaker flattening (outliers can't be spread across the whole vector), so rotation quality < full Hadamard.\n\nAlso randomized Hadamard (random signs ⊙ H) for decorrelation of specific channels, seeded — need same H shared across arms.\n\nLet me run one more check computing: for each model, block-diag feasibility B=g=128 on rotation-relevant dims; and also the interaction between group boundaries and Hadamard blocks (if B == g and blocks aligned with groups, the rotation mixes weights within a group only — that means the group's scale statistics change but no cross-group leakage; alternatively B = k·g to mix across groups, requiring group re-quantization... Actually important design detail: rotation is applied to the weight matrix along input dim; then grouping is done on the rotated matrix, so groups are defined post-rotation. Whether B aligns with g matters for the equivalence claim \"same effective bits\" — actually effective bits are the same regardless. But alignment matters for the interpretation: with B = g, rotation only redistributes magnitude within each group; that's exactly what helps