nd that a single-spike vector is spread by Hadamard is linear algebra, not a model result. I'll keep it as a math identity check, clearly labeled, and note that quantization-error consequences are hypotheses, not measurements. Actually, careful: even a synthetic demo showing \"spread\" is fine as long as I don't extrapolate it to real models' perplexity. I'll include a minimal Sylvester orthogonality check.\n\nThen write the full design as stage=result.\n\nLet me run the final checks.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\nMODELS = {\n \"llama2-7b\": dict(h=4096, ffn=11008, layers=32), \"llama2-13b\": dict(h=5120, ffn=13824, layers=40),\n \"llama3-8b\": dict(h=4096, ffn=14336, layers=32), \"llama2-70b\": dict(h=8192, ffn=28672, layers=80),\n \"opt-1.3b\": dict(h=2048, ffn=8192, layers=24), \"opt-6.7b\": dict(h=4096, ffn=16384, layers=32),\n}\nB=128\nprint(f\"=== BLOCK-DIAGONAL Hadamard with FIXED block B={B} on rotation-relevant input dims ===\")\nfor n,m in MODELS.items():\n    t = (m[\"h\"]%B==0, m[\"ffn\"]%B==0)\n    print(f\"  {n:11s} d_model={m['h']:6d} ({m['h']//B:4d} blocks) d_ff={m['ffn']:6d} ({m['ffn']//B:4d} blocks) tiles_both={all(t)}\")\nprint(\"  -> alignment with groups g in {64,128,256}: g=64 -> 64|B (group inside one block);\")\nprint(\"     g=128 -> g==B (group == block); g=256 -> B|g (2 blocks per group). All aligned, no straddling.\")\nprint()\ndef sylvester(n):\n    H=[[1]]\n    while len(H)<n:\n        H=[r+[v for v in r] for r in H]+[[v for v in r]+[-v for v in r] for r in H]\n    return H\nH=sylvester(B)\nI=[[(H[r][c] if False else 0) for c in range(B)] for r in range(B)]\ngram=[[sum(H[r][k]*H[c][k] for k in range(B)) for c in range(B)] for r in range(B)]\noff=sum(1 for r in range(B) for c in range(B) if gram[r][c]!=(B if r==c else 0))\nprint(f\"=== SYLVESTER H_{B} orthogonality (exact integer arithmetic): H H^T = {B}*I ? violations={off}\")\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\n# math 