each transformer block; folded offline into W_qkv, W_gate/up and into the previous layer's W_o/W_down output side).\n- R2/R3: per-head Hadamard on V and on o_proj input (V-cache and attention output).\n- R4: online Hadamard on activations right after nonlinearity (SiLU) and after attention softmax → the down_proj and o_proj input side; these are \"online\" because the nonlinearity/softmax breaks the invariance chain.\n- R5: online Hadamard on the K cache / query (for K-V cache quantization).\n\nIn weight-only (activations FP16):\n- Any online Hadamard applied to FP16 activations is mathematically a no-op for accuracy IF the corresponding weight was multiplied by the same Hadamard offline... wait careful. Let's think clearly.\n\nSetup: We want the network function f(x;W). QuaRot's rotations preserve the exact function: for W' = W R (with R orthogonal) and x' = x R^T, x'W'^T = x R^T R W^T = x W^T. Exact. Quantizing W' instead of W changes the rounding geometry: quant error in the rotated basis, which when mapped back is x R^T E^T where E is rounding error of the rotated weights. The point: rotation makes W's columns better-conditioned (flatter dynamic range within a quantization group) → smaller E in the rotated basis; because R is orthogonal, ||E R^T|| = ||E||, so the error norm equals the rounding error norm; the win comes from lower rounding error in the rotated basis.\n\nBut important: to apply the rotation in weight-only, you MUST also apply R^T to the activations at inference (online), otherwise the function is wrong. This costs FLOPs but not accuracy. Alternatively, R can be pushed back through preceding linear layers and folded there (offline, into the previous weight matrices), avoiding the online cost — that's exactly what QuaRot does with R1 (folded into LN+previous matrices). But note: folding rotation into preceding weight matrices changes THOSE matrices, which are themselves quantized. So the rotation choice propagates through the quantized weight set. This matters: an \"offline fold\" of the rotation into a preceding quantized matrix changes the quantization grid of that matrix too (usually beneficial: this is how the rotation actually smooths the weight distribution of the folded matrix).\n\nSo per-cell traceability, in weight-only mode:\n- R1 (residual-stream Hadamard at block input, folded into LN+embed/out-proj side, applied to W_qkv, W_gate/up): ACTIVE (offline fold). Justification: RMSNorm commutes with rotation? Careful: QuaRot folds LN weight into the rotation: LayerNorm weight diag folded into W, and RMSNorm scaling is invariant to an orthogonal transform of the input? RMSNorm(xR) = RMSNorm(x)R only because RMSNorm normalizes by ||x|| and R orthogonal → ||xR|| = ||x||, and the row-wise normalization commutes. Yes, RMSNorm(x R) = x R / rms(x) * ... careful with LN weight; fold LN weight into R: diag(g) R = diag(g') ... actually QuaRot does exactly this (merges LN weight into R1). So justified.\n- R4 (online Hadamards after softmax and after SiLU activation, i.e., on inputs to W_o and W_down): In weight-only, if we apply R4 to W_o/W_down input dim we must also apply R4 online to the FP16 activations. Since activations are FP16 and the product is exact up to FP rounding, the net effect on the function is identity, BUT the quantized matrix changes: W_down' = W_down R4^T quantized, then activation a' = a R4. The error is E R4^T-ish; since a is FP16 exact, the only error is rounding of the rotated matrix. So YES, R4 on the weight input side IS meaningful in weight-only mode — it smooths W_down/W_o input columns. Wait — but does it? The input dim of W_down is the FFN intermediate dim, whose activations are SiLU outputs (non-negative-ish, skewed). Quantizing W_down's input dim (grouped along input dim) benefits from rotating the columns of W_down. But we also need the online R4 to keep the function exact. That's a real online cost, but for weight-only inference the \"activation\" is FP16, so applying a Hadamard to FP16 activations is an extra FLOPs cost but exact. Hmm, but actually there's a subtlety: in a pure weight-only quantized model (weights int, activations FP16), applying the online Hadamard to FP16 activations is an extra op that costs time/memory bandwidth — it's not free but is exact. Alternatively we could fold R4 into the preceding quantized weight (W_up/W_gate) — no, can't because of the SiLU nonlinearity in between (which is why QuaRot uses online Hadamard for that one). So in weight-only, R4 must be applied online. Decision: include it, but as an ablation (R4-on vs R4-offline-folded-only) because it changes inference cost, and note that the extra cost must be reported. Actually, the researcher wants the design; I'll include a sub-ablation: \"rotation depth\" levels:\n  - Rot-L0 (no rotation) = baselines.\n  - Rot-L1 (only offline-foldable rotations: R1 residual-stream folded; K-V/per-head rotations folded where they don't require online ops) → zero online cost, pure weight-space transform.\n  - Rot-L2 (full: + R4 online Hadamards on FP16 activations for W_o/W_down input dims) → full QuaRot weight-space treatment; extra online FLOPs.\n  This is a nice, auditable decomposition and it directly addresses the researcher's matiz.\n- K/V cache rotations (R5) and per-head V rotation: ANULADAS in weight-only, justification: cache is FP16, quantizing nothing there → no grid to change; R5 applied to FP16 K and Q cancels (Q R)(K R)^T = Q K^T. So no-op, disabled by design (saves compute), and this is not an assumption but a consequence of the weight-only requirement. Except: V-per-head rotation folded into W_v output dim and W_o input dim... The V rotation R2 folds into W_v (output dim) and W_o (input dim); since W_v's output dim rotation doesn't change W_v's quantization grid much (grid is along input dim groups), but W_o's input dim IS rotated → that's part of R4/o_proj. So: per-head V rotation affects only W_o's quantization grid, which we get via the o_proj rotation anyway. State: the V-side rotations reduce, in weight-only, to the o_proj input rotation; the K-side rotations are exact no-ops (disabled). Good, that's a clean and checkable algebraic argument.\n- Embedding/lm_head: excluded from quantization (structural list). Also lm_head quantization is excluded in QuaRot typically; keep as structural exclusion in all arms.\n\nImportant: \"outliers\" — no FP16 rescue. The rotation IS the outlier treatment.\n\nEffective bits: with block-diag Hadamard B=128 and g=128 → groups align exactly with rotation blocks (nice). 32-bit extra per group? I assumed 16-bit scale + 16-bit zero → 4.25. For symmetric, 16-bit scale + 4-bit? Let's just spec: symmetric quantization (scales FP16, no zero-point for symmetric; but QuaRot/GPTQ often asymmetric). Design decision: use asymmetric group quant with FP16 scale + FP16 zero → 4.25/3.25/2.25 effective bits, identical across all arms (already computed). I'll present both options but fix asymmetric g=128 as primary and note symmetric as robustness check (a single extra grid point difference). Hmm, to keep it simple: primary = asymmetric, per-group, FP16 scale+zero, 4.25/3.25/2.25 effective. Also report per-group bit overhead honestly.\n\nMetrics & analysis:\n- primary: ppl WikiText-2 test (seq 2048, non-overlapping, 2048/2047 tokenization shift), C4 validation (first 256 sequences? specify: fixed 256×2048 sample, same for all arms).\n- degradation ratio DR = ppl_q / ppl_fp16 per (model, arm, bits).\n- collapse: DR > 2.0 (pre-registered, fixed now).\n- secondary: zero-shot suite lm-eval: PIQA, ARC-e, ARC-c, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, LAMBADA-openai, plus TruthfulQA? keep 6 tasks; report mean over 6 and per-task; and macro-average normalized accuracy retention = acc_q/acc_fp16.\n- diagnostics: (i) per-layer relative quant MSE: ||W̃-W||_F²/||W||_F², aggregated per block and total; (ii) KL(FP16 || quant) over next-token distributions on a fixed 32×2048-token eval subset, mean per token; (iii) outlier stats: